 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Proportional to the number of voxels traversed by the ray. In the worst case, this is $O(N_r + N_p + N_a)$ where $N_r, N_p, N_a$ are the number of radial, polar, and azimuthal sections [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N_r + N_p + N_a)$ to store the output voxel list [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Efficiently handles spherical symmetry and avoids the distortion artifacts of Cartesian grids when rendering spherical volumes. It correctly handles the topology of spherical coordinates [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Complex implementation due to the need to handle multiple intersection types and boundary conditions. Performance degrades if the ray passes near the poles or center where voxel sizes shrink [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use**: When rendering volumetric data that is naturally defined in spherical coordinates (e.g., planetary atmospheres, spherical clouds) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity**: Linear in the number of voxels traversed. Each step involves constant-time geometric calculations for boundary intersections [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the number of voxels traversed, stored in a vector. Precomputed grid data (trig values, boundary segments) is stored in the grid structure [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Exact boundary intersection for spherical grids, avoiding the approximation errors of Cartesian voxel traversal on spherical domains. Efficient for rendering spherical volumes like atmospheres [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Complex implementation due to handling multiple intersection types and edge cases (collinear hits, boundary bounds). Performance degrades if the ray traverses a large number of small voxels [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The algorithm was introduced in the context of spherical volume rendering, specifically in the `svr-algorithm` repository [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the need for efficient traversal of non-Cartesian voxel grids, which are common in scientific visualization of spherical domains [S1, unofficial].
+The algorithm is part of the Spherical Volume Rendering (SVR) project, which aims to provide efficient rendering techniques for volumetric data on spherical domains [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of Cartesian ray marching when applied to spherical geometries, where boundary handling and distortion are significant issues [S1, unofficial].
 
